---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Best Unicode to Preeti Converter — What to Look For (2026)';
const description =
  'Comparing Unicode to Preeti converters on accuracy, privacy, file support and price. Run the 30-second conjunct test on any converter before you trust it.';

const keywords = [
  'best unicode to preeti converter',
  'unicode to preeti converter comparison',
  'which unicode to preeti converter is best',
  'unicode to preeti converter online',
  'free unicode to preeti converter',
].join(', ');

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'unicodeTOpreeti' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'unicodeTOpreeti' },
  mainEntityOfPage: 'https://unicodetopreeti.xyz/best-unicode-to-preeti-converter',
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  keywords={keywords}
  ogType="article"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="hangs intro">
      <p class="crumb"><a href="/guides">Guides</a> /</p>
      <h1>Best Unicode to Preeti Converter — What to Look For</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every Unicode to Preeti converter looks the same until you paste a hard
        word into it. Here is how to judge them in 30 seconds, and how the
        popular options compare on the things that actually matter.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="hangs hangs--thin">
      <h2>The 30-second accuracy test</h2>
      <p>
        Unicode stores Nepali in logical order (consonant, then vowel sign)
        while Preeti stores it the way a typewriter draws it. Simple
        character-by-character converters get three cases wrong. Paste these
        three words into any converter:
      </p>
      <ul class="test-words">
        <li><span class="devanagari">किसान</span> — the i-matra must jump left of क</li>
        <li><span class="devanagari">कर्म</span> — the reph must ride above म</li>
        <li><span class="devanagari">क्क</span> — half क plus full क, not two full कs</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If all three come out right, the converter works syllable by syllable
        and you can trust it with real documents. If any one scrambles, every
        long document you run through it will need manual fixing. This is the
        single most important test — more important than design, speed or extra
        features.
      </p>

      <h2>What else matters</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Privacy.</strong> You are often converting office documents,
          exam papers or personal letters. A converter that runs entirely in
          your browser never sends your text to a server.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Both directions.</strong> Old Preeti documents need to come
          back to Unicode too. A one-way converter only solves half the job.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>File support.</strong> For long documents, uploading a .txt
          file and downloading the converted result beats copy-paste.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Limits and price.</strong> Some converters cap characters or
          require accounts. For a text-mapping task there is no reason to
          accept either.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How the popular options compare</h2>
      <p>
        Features change, so treat this as a snapshot at the time of writing.
        The accuracy test above is the tiebreaker whenever two converters both
        claim to handle conjuncts.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Converter</th>
              <th scope="col">Accuracy approach</th>
              <th scope="col">File upload</th>
              <th scope="col">Both directions</th>
              <th scope="col">Offline / private</th>
              <th scope="col">Signup / limits</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><a href="/">unicodeTOpreeti (this site)</a></th>
              <td>Most accurate, self-formatting: syllable-level engine; i-matra, reph and conjuncts auto-reordered; verified by 401 automated conversion checks</td>
              <td>.txt upload &amp; download</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>Yes — runs in your browser</td>
              <td>None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">merokalam.com</th>
              <td>General converter</td>
              <td>Yes — .txt/.docx upload, DOC export</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>No — server-side</td>
              <td>Free</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">barala.com.np</th>
              <td>General converter</td>
              <td>No</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>No — server-side</td>
              <td>Free</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">preetitounicode.com</th>
              <td>General converter</td>
              <td>Yes — .docx/.pdf upload</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>No — server-side</td>
              <td>Free</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Our honest recommendation</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Need .docx or PDF upload with layout preserved?</strong> Use a
          converter that offers it, such as preetitounicode.com — then run the
          30-second test on the result.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Converting Kantipur?</strong>
          Use the <a href="/kantipur-to-preeti">Kantipur to Preeti converter</a> on this site, which uses a dedicated Kantipur mapping.
          <strong>Converting Sagarmatha or another legacy font?</strong>
          You need a converter built for that font's mapping — barala.com.np
          covers several legacy fonts. A Preeti converter will not do.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Converting Unicode to Preeti (or back) and want it right?</strong>
          Use the <a href="/">converter on this site</a>: most accurate,
          self-formatting syllable-level accuracy, both directions, .txt file
          support, no signup, no limits, and your text never leaves your device.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Whichever you choose, run the three-word test first. Thirty seconds
        now saves hours of fixing garbled documents later.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .intro {
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .crumb {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .crumb a {
    color: var(--crimson);
  }

  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding: 0.05em 0.3em;
  }

  .test-words {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }

  .test-words li {
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--crimson);
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 1rem;
  }

  .test-words .devanagari {
    font-size: 1.2em;
    margin-right: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
  }

  .compare {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    min-width: 640px;
  }

  .compare th,
  .compare td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.65rem 0.8rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  .compare thead th {
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .compare tbody th {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .hangs ul:not(.test-words) {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }

  .hangs li + li {
    margin-top: 0.55rem;
  }

  .hangs p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    line-height: 1.75;
  }
</style>
